# Sine of `num` radians as `number`.
#
# The angle is reduced twice: whole turns come off it first, which leaves
# it in [-pi, pi], and then quarter turns, which leaves it in
# [-pi/4, pi/4]. The answer is the sine or the cosine of what is left, with
# the sign the quarter asks for. One reduction is not enough: an angle near
# a multiple of pi lands at the edge of [-pi, pi], where the Taylor series
# of the sine cancels its leading digits and keeps almost none of them, so
# `pi.sine` came back as `6.98e-16` against the `1.22e-16` it is, and
# `355.sine` was two hundred thousand of its last places out (#9279).
#
# What is reduced is the magnitude, and the sign of the argument is put
# back on the answer, so that the sine of a negative angle is exactly the
# negation of the sine of its magnitude. Reducing the argument itself is
# not odd, because `floor` is not: `pi` lost a whole turn where `-pi` lost
# none, and the two were summed at opposite ends of the range.
#
# The reduction takes `turns` whole turns off the magnitude, and the
# accuracy of the answer is the accuracy of that one subtraction: at `1e15`
# the reduced angle is what survives of a number sixteen digits long, so a
# product of `turns` and two pi rounded into a single double keeps none of
# the digits that decide the sine. The product is therefore carried in two
# doubles.
#
# `tau` is the nearest double to two pi and `tail` is what that double
# misses. `turns times tau` is taken exactly by Dekker's method: `gap` is
# two to the 27th plus one, and multiplying by it and subtracting back
# splits a double into halves narrow enough that their pairwise products
# round nowhere: `turns` into `top` and `foot`, `tau` into `crown` and
# `stub`. The four partials `high`, `mid`, `cross` and `low` then say what
# the single rounded `product` dropped, and `slip` is that dropped part.
#
# What the whole turns leave is carried in two doubles as well, rather than
# folded into one. `coarse` is the magnitude less the rounded product,
# which is exact, since the two stand a few units apart; `fine` is
# everything the correction still owes it. `angle` is their difference and
# is read for one thing only, to say which quarter the angle is in, as
# `steps`. The quarter turn itself comes off `coarse`, before `fine` is
# paid: that subtraction is exact too, by Sterbenz, so `rest` keeps every
# digit of an angle near a multiple of pi/2, while a single rounded `angle`
# would have left it with the width of its own last place, which is all of
# a sine of `1e-5`. `quarter` is a quarter of `tau` and `speck` a quarter
# of `tail`, both exact, since dividing by four only moves an exponent.
#
# `sined` is the Taylor series of the sine over `rest` and `cosined` that
# of the cosine, both summed through Horner's scheme, and `picked` reads
# one of the two off `steps`: the sine in the quarter around zero, the
# cosine in the one above it, and the negation of either on the far side,
# since a sine shifted by two quarters is the negation of itself.
#
# The attributes below stand in alphabetical order, which is not the order
# they are computed in; the paragraphs above are the order.
#
# Two doubles carry about 106 bits, so the reduction holds to a few ULP for
# as long as `turns times two pi` needs no more than that, which is |num| up
# to `1e15`. Past it the reduced angle drifts out of [-pi, pi] and the series
# is summed so far from zero that it answers outside [-1, 1], which is no
# sine of anything: `1e300` came back as infinity. A finite angle above
# `1e15` therefore terminates, rather than answering a number a sine cannot
# be. Holding the whole range of a double would take Payne-Hanek reduction,
# with two-over-pi written out to more bits than any product of doubles
# carries. Non-finite arguments collapse to nan, as they did.

+architect yegor256@gmail.com
+home https://github.com/objectionary/eo
+package number
+spdx SPDX-FileCopyrightText: Copyright (c) 2016-2026 Objectionary.com
+spdx SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
+version 0.0.0

[^num] > sine
  if. > @
    arg.is-finite.and
      arg.abs.gt 1000000000000000
    T "Can't take a sine of an angle above 1e15, the reduction cannot hold it"
    if.
      arg.as-bytes.eq 80-00-00-00-00-00-00-00
      arg
      if.
        arg.lt 0
        picked.neg
        picked
  coarse.minus fine > angle!
  number num.as-bytes > arg
  spread.minus tau > away
  magnitude.minus product > coarse
  1.minus > cosined
    times.
      div.
        small.times small
        2
      if. > [^ depth] >> cosine
        depth.gt 15
        1
        1.minus
          times.
            div.
              ^.small.times ^.small
              times.
                minus.
                  depth.times 2
                  1
                depth.times 2
            ^.cosine
              depth.plus 1
      | 2
  foot.times crown > cross
  spread.minus away > crown
  slip.plus > fine
    turns.times tail
  turns.minus top > foot
  134217729 > gap
  top.times crown > high
  foot.times stub > low
  arg.abs > magnitude
  top.times stub > mid
  if. > picked
    steps.eq 0
    sined
    if.
      steps.eq 1
      cosined
      if.
        steps.eq -1
        cosined.neg
        sined.neg
  turns.times tau > product
  tau.div 4 > quarter
  number angle > reduced
  minus. > rest!
    minus.
      coarse.minus
        steps.times quarter
      fine
    steps.times speck
  wide.minus turns > shed
  small.times > sined
    if. > [^ depth] >> factor
      depth.gt 15
      1
      1.minus
        times.
          div.
            ^.small.times ^.small
            times.
              depth.times 2
              plus.
                depth.times 2
                1
          ^.factor
            depth.plus 1
    | 1
  plus. > slip
    plus.
      plus.
        high.minus product
        mid
      cross
    low
  number rest > small
  tail.div 4 > speck
  tau.times gap > spread
  floor. > steps
    plus.
      reduced.div quarter
      0.5
  tau.minus crown > stub
  2.4492935982947064e-16 > tail
  pi.times 2 > tau
  wide.minus shed > top
  floor. > turns
    plus.
      magnitude.div tau
      0.5
  turns.times gap > wide

  lt. ++> can-be-accurate-near-the-documented-four-hundred-million-boundary
    abs.
      400000000.sine.minus 0.9965407850188008
    1.0e-9

  pi.sine.eq pi.neg.sine.neg ++> can-be-exactly-odd-at-pi

  lt. ++> can-behave-as-an-odd-function-when-taking-sine
    abs.
      times.
        plus.
          sine.
            pi.div 3
          sine. (pi.div 3).neg
        1000
    1

  lt. ++> can-reduce-a-large-angle-when-taking-sine
    abs.
      minus.
        times.
          sine.
            plus.
              pi.times 10
              pi.div 6
          1000
        500
    1

  lt. ++> can-reduce-a-very-large-angle-with-cody-waite-precision
    abs.
      minus.
        1000000000000000.sine.times 1000
        858
    1

  nan.sine.is-nan ++> can-return-nan-for-a-sine-of-nan

  ninf.sine.is-nan ++> can-return-nan-for-a-sine-of-negative-infinity

  pinf.sine.is-nan ++> can-return-nan-for-a-sine-of-positive-infinity

  lt. ++> can-take-a-sine-just-below-pi
    abs.
      minus.
        div.
          sine.
            pi.minus 1.0e-6
          1.000000000262076e-6
        1
    1.0e-14

  lt. ++> can-take-a-sine-of-a-quadrillion
    abs.
      1000000000000000.sine.minus 0.8582727931702359
    1.0e-14

  lt. ++> can-take-a-sine-of-minus-a-quadrillion
    abs.
      -1000000000000000.sine.plus 0.8582727931702359
    1.0e-14

  lt. ++> can-take-a-sine-of-minus-pi-halves
    abs.
      minus.
        times. (pi.div 2).neg.sine 1000
        -1000
    1

  eq. ++> can-take-a-sine-of-minus-zero-preserving-sign
    0.neg.sine.as-bytes
    80-00-00-00-00-00-00-00

  lt. ++> can-take-a-sine-of-pi
    abs.
      pi.sine.times 1000
    1

  lt. ++> can-take-a-sine-of-pi-halves
    abs.
      minus.
        times.
          sine.
            pi.div 2
          1000
        1000
    1

  lt. ++> can-take-a-sine-of-pi-sixths
    abs.
      minus.
        times.
          sine.
            pi.div 6
          1000
        500
    1

  lt. ++> can-take-a-sine-of-pi-to-its-last-places
    abs.
      pi.sine.minus 1.2246467991473532e-16
    1.0e-31

  lt. ++> can-take-a-sine-of-seventeen-radians
    abs.
      minus.
        17.sine.times 1000
        -961
    1

  lt. ++> can-take-a-sine-of-three-hundred-and-fifty-five
    abs.
      minus.
        355.sine.div -3.014435335948845e-5
        1
    1.0e-14

  lt. ++> can-take-a-sine-of-three-pi-halves
    abs.
      minus.
        times.
          sine.
            div.
              pi.times 3
              2
          1000
        -1000
    1

  lt. ++> can-take-a-sine-of-two-pi
    abs.
      times.
        sine.
          pi.times 2
        1000
    1

  0.sine.eq 0 ++> can-take-a-sine-of-zero

  1.0e300.sine --> stops-on-a-sine-of-an-angle-the-reduction-cannot-hold
